<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narmer Palette</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3100 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a> Palette is a carved siltstone palette of about 3100 BCE found in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nekhen">Hierakonpolis</a> (Nekhen) in 1897–1898. It shows King Narmer wearing the white crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> on one side and the red crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> on the other, smiting an enemy and inspecting slain foes. It is often read as an early record of the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and is kept in the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narmer-palette.jpg" alt="Narmer Palette" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narmer_Palette.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
